# ns-O-RAN / ns-3 spectrum: cache the long-term beamforming component PER BEAM.
# Built as nsg-engine:1.26-ltcache. NOT in the demo image (1.25) — see the end.
#
# THE MEASUREMENT CHAIN THAT LED HERE
#
# 1. The 1-link floor is only ~2x from real time, so the ns-3 event machinery is
#    not the wall (nsg-cost-is-per-ue-not-pairs).
# 2. Cost grows with UEs, not cells: 12x the cells costs 2.76x, 4x the UEs costs
#    5.87x. So link pruning is closed and something per-UE is super-linear.
# 3. Two call-graph profiles at the same cell count, 3 vs 12 UEs, ~18.6k samples
#    each: `CalculateLongTermComponent` is absent from the top of the 3-UE
#    profile and is 43.25% of the 12-UE one (37.76% self). Meanwhile
#    `GetNewChannel` COLLAPSES, 35.74% -> 5.95%.
# 4. That collapse is the discriminator. GetLongTerm's cache is dropped when the
#    channel regenerates OR a beam changes; channel regeneration is happening
#    LESS, so the beam must be doing it.
# 5. Counters confirmed it. Per million lookups, 3 cells:
#        3 UEs : hit 996,949  miss_beam 1,881    miss_channel 1,155
#       12 UEs : hit 871,892  miss_beam 327,163  miss_channel 840
#    4x the UEs, 174x the beam misses, and channel misses FLAT. 99.7% of all
#    misses at 12 UEs are beam misses.
#
# WHY IT HAPPENS
# mmwave-enb-phy.cc re-points the beam at the scheduled UE every TTI. Upstream
# keeps ONE long-term per tx-rx pair, so every switch evicts it — and the
# scheduler cycles a bounded set of beams, so it is recomputed for a beam whose
# long term was already computed moments earlier. Cells do not change how often
# the beam switches; UEs do. That is why cells are cheap and UEs are not.
#
# WHAT THIS CHANGES
# One slot per pair becomes a small set keyed by (channel generation, sW, uW).
# Same inputs, same outputs, fewer recomputations. A regenerated channel still
# clears every variant for the pair.
#
# MEASURED, 3 cells / 12 UEs, 120 s window, 2 seeds:
#   speed     span 2.35 -> 2.95  =  1.26x
#   fidelity  1,201 RSRP samples matched by (t, cell, imsi) across an early and
#             a late window: 1,201 identical, 0 differing.
#
# That fidelity check is the point. Every other speed lever measured on this
# engine traded accuracy and still stalled at ~1.3x (nsg-speed-levers-measured);
# this one changes no number. And the gain should GROW with UE count, since beam
# misses grow super-linearly — unmeasured above 12 UEs.
#
# A TRAP THIS PATCH COST ME: comparing averages between the two builds looked
# like a fidelity REGRESSION (seed 1 throughput 12063 -> 10078). It was the span
# confound — the faster build reached 3.3 simulated seconds against 2.4, so the
# two averages cover different intervals. Compare per-sample by key, never
# run-level means, and never by line order: the RIC and xApp run on real sockets,
# so event ordering shifts between runs of the SAME build.
#
# NOT SHIPPED TO THE DEMO. The demo runs 1.25, verified and unchanged. This is
# hours old and measured at one config; promote it after the demo with wider
# verification (more UE counts, more seeds, and the fidelity check re-run).
#
# Apply in the engine distro:
#   cd /home/builder/ns-O-RAN-flexric/mmwave-LENA-oran && patch -p0 < this
#   runuser -u builder -- ./ns3 build scenario-zero-with_parallel_loging
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--- /home/builder/ns-O-RAN-flexric/mmwave-LENA-oran/src/spectrum/model/three-gpp-spectrum-propagation-loss-model.cc.pre_ltcache	2026-07-07 01:05:27.932310522 +0300
+++ /home/builder/ns-O-RAN-flexric/mmwave-LENA-oran/src/spectrum/model/three-gpp-spectrum-propagation-loss-model.cc	2026-09-17 14:56:28.007603322 +0300
@@ -462,18 +462,36 @@
     uint64_t longTermId =
         MatrixBasedChannelModel::GetKey(aPhasedArrayModel->GetId(), bPhasedArrayModel->GetId());
 
-    // look for the long term in the map and check if it is valid
-    if (m_longTermMap.find(longTermId) != m_longTermMap.end())
+    // NSG: look for a long term computed for THIS beam pair, not just this
+    // tx-rx pair. Upstream keeps one slot per pair and drops it whenever either
+    // beamforming vector changes — but the gNB re-points its beam at the
+    // scheduled UE every TTI, so with several UEs on a cell the slot is thrown
+    // away and recomputed as the scheduler cycles back to a beam it already had.
+    //
+    // A regenerated channel matrix still invalidates EVERY variant for the pair,
+    // so the generation time is checked once against the set rather than per
+    // entry — which also keeps every entry on one generation.
+    auto ltIt = m_longTermMap.find(longTermId);
+    if (ltIt != m_longTermMap.end() && !ltIt->second.empty())
     {
-        NS_LOG_DEBUG("found the long term component in the map");
-        longTerm = m_longTermMap[longTermId]->m_longTerm;
-
-        // check if the channel matrix has been updated
-        // or the s beam has been changed
-        // or the u beam has been changed
-        update = (m_longTermMap[longTermId]->m_channel->m_generatedTime !=
-                      channelMatrix->m_generatedTime ||
-                  m_longTermMap[longTermId]->m_sW != sW || m_longTermMap[longTermId]->m_uW != uW);
+        if (ltIt->second.front()->m_channel->m_generatedTime !=
+            channelMatrix->m_generatedTime)
+        {
+            ltIt->second.clear(); // the channel moved: every beam variant is stale
+            notFound = true;
+        }
+        else
+        {
+            for (const auto& cached : ltIt->second)
+            {
+                if (cached->m_sW == sW && cached->m_uW == uW)
+                {
+                    NS_LOG_DEBUG("found the long term component in the map");
+                    return cached->m_longTerm;
+                }
+            }
+            notFound = true; // this pair is cached, but not for this beam
+        }
     }
     else
     {
@@ -489,12 +507,19 @@
         Ptr<LongTerm> longTermItem = Create<LongTerm>();
         longTermItem->m_longTerm = longTerm;
         longTermItem->m_channel = channelMatrix;
-        longTermItem->m_sW = std::move(sW);
-        longTermItem->m_uW = std::move(uW);
-        // store the long term to reduce computation load
-        // only the small scale fading needs to be updated if the large scale parameters and antenna
-        // weights remain unchanged.
-        m_longTermMap[longTermId] = longTermItem;
+        // NOT moved: the vectors are the cache KEY now and are compared on every
+        // later lookup, so the entry has to keep its own copy.
+        longTermItem->m_sW = sW;
+        longTermItem->m_uW = uW;
+        // Store this beam's long term alongside the pair's other beams. Oldest
+        // out when full — the scheduler cycles a bounded set, so the working set
+        // is the number of UEs the cell serves.
+        auto& variants = m_longTermMap[longTermId];
+        if (variants.size() >= NSG_LT_VARIANTS)
+        {
+            variants.erase(variants.begin());
+        }
+        variants.push_back(longTermItem);
     }
 
     return longTerm;
